# Agent Wolf — local topology config.
#
# START ORANGE FIRST. Agent Wolf's compose file joins Orange's compose
# network as EXTERNAL (agent-orange_default) and shares Orange's DinD
# container's network namespace — both must already exist. See
# design/2026-08-20-agent-wolf.md § "Local topology and networking"
# (agent-orange repo) for why: agentd shares DinD's netns in the standalone
# stack, so a naive wolf-api sitting on the ordinary compose network would be
# unreachable from session containers.
#
#   cd ../agent-orange && cp .env.example .env && docker compose up --build
#   cd ../agent-wolf   && cp .env.example .env && docker compose up --build

# ── Topology (docker-compose.yml only — read by Docker, not by wolf-api) ─────

# Name of Agent Orange's dind container. wolf-api runs with
# `network_mode: "container:${ORANGE_DIND_CONTAINER}"`, so it shares that
# container's network namespace: it reaches agentd at http://localhost:8099
# (same netns) and is reachable FROM session containers at
# http://172.17.0.1:${WOLF_API_PORT} (the DinD bridge gateway — the address
# nested session containers actually resolve). Default matches the compose
# project name "agent-orange" (the directory name) with the default
# `docker compose` naming: <project>-<service>-<replica>.
ORANGE_DIND_CONTAINER=agent-orange-dind-1

# Port wolf-web (nginx) publishes on the host. Open http://localhost:$WOLF_WEB_PORT.
WOLF_WEB_PORT=8081

# Port wolf-api listens on, INSIDE the shared dind netns (not a host port —
# wolf-api publishes no host port of its own; only wolf-web does). Read by
# wolf-api itself (api/src/config.ts) to bind its HTTP server, and by
# wolf-web's nginx template to build its proxy_pass target
# (http://dind:${WOLF_API_PORT}), and by session containers' MCP config
# (http://172.17.0.1:${WOLF_API_PORT}/mcp, i.e. WOLF_MCP_URL below).
WOLF_API_PORT=8100

# ── wolf-api process config (api/src/config.ts) ───────────────────────────────

# The value written into the project's MCP config so a session container can
# reach wolf-api's /mcp endpoint. In THIS compose stack that is the DinD
# bridge address, never a compose service name (nested containers cannot
# resolve compose DNS) and never localhost (that would resolve inside the
# session container itself). In a real (non-compose) deployment both
# services are ordinary hosts and this is just wolf-api's normal /mcp URL —
# the variable exists so nothing hard-codes either form.
#
# Read directly by api/src/config.ts (resolveMcpUrl), not just by Docker:
# READ THIS BEFORE UNCOMMENTING. Left unset (the default here), wolf-api
# DISCOVERS the DinD bridge address itself at boot. It reads DinD's own
# docker0 bridge — the `docker0` row of /proc/net/route, whose network + 1
# is the bridge itself — and NEVER the default route: under compose the
# default route is the OUTER compose-network gateway, an address no session
# container can reach. (W33/R235: the probe did read the default route, and
# every mcp__wolf__* call from inside a session failed with nowhere to
# connect. The two addresses agree on a plain Docker install and differ
# under compose, which is why nothing caught it until the stack was run.)
# It falls back to the literal 172.17.0.1 only if that read finds no
# docker0 row, because that address is a Docker default, not a constant: it
# becomes e.g. 172.18.0.1 whenever the 172.17.0.0/16 subnet is already
# taken on the host.
#
# An EMPTY value counts as unset. docker-compose.yml forwards
# `${WOLF_MCP_URL:-}`, so an operator who has not set it gets "" inside the
# container — and discovery still runs, which is the only reason the
# compose default works at all.
#
# Setting this variable explicitly WINS OUTRIGHT over discovery, so only set
# it if you know the gateway will not move (a real, non-compose deployment,
# where both services are ordinary hosts) — in the compose stack, leave it
# unset and let wolf-api discover it; check the "resolved WOLF_MCP_URL" boot
# log line to see which path was used.
# WOLF_MCP_URL=http://172.17.0.1:8100/mcp

# pino log level: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace | silent.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# development | production | test.
NODE_ENV=production

# ── Market data + the wolf MCP server (api/src/config.ts, api/src/mcp/) ──────

# The credential a session container presents to wolf-api's /mcp endpoint,
# in the BARE header `X-Wolf-Mcp-Token: <token>` — no `Bearer` prefix, ever:
# Orange stores an MCP header value as a whole-value ${VAR} reference or a
# literal, never a mix, so "Bearer ${WOLF_MCP_TOKEN}" cannot be stored at
# all. It is a DIFFERENT credential from WOLF_API_KEY (which authenticates
# wolf-api TO Orange) and it must be the same value on both sides: here, and
# in the `wolf` project's MCP config that reaches the container through
# Orange's AGENTKIT_MCP_ENV.
#
# REQUIRED: wolf-api refuses to boot without it rather than serve its
# market-data tools unauthenticated. 32-128 characters of [A-Za-z0-9_-]
# (URL-safe base64) — no spaces, quotes or `$`, which are how a value
# travelling through a shell export, Docker and Orange's ${VAR}
# interpolation breaks silently. Generate one, and NEVER commit the value:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
WOLF_MCP_TOKEN=

# The HMAC key that signs /series/download URLs (`series_fetch` mints them;
# the route verifies them). At least 32 characters.
#
# OPTIONAL: left unset, wolf-api generates a fresh random secret at boot.
# That is a real default, not a weak one — there is no shared constant to
# leak. The only consequence is that URLs minted before a restart stop
# verifying after it, which the short TTL below makes near-harmless. Set it
# only if more than one wolf-api process must honour each other's URLs.
# WOLF_SERIES_TOKEN_SECRET=

# How long a minted /series/download URL stays valid, in whole SECONDS
# (1-3600, default 300). Keep it short: the URL carries its token into the
# model's context, the persisted transcript and every worker.finished
# subscriber — the same caveat the design states for Orange's dataset_get.
# WOLF_SERIES_URL_TTL_SECONDS=300

# FRED (St. Louis Fed) API key, for the macro half of the market-data tools.
# Free and instant from https://fredaccount.stlouisfed.org/apikeys — the
# KEYED JSON API is the only one with a /fred/series/search endpoint, which
# series_search needs.
#
# OPTIONAL: unset, wolf-api still boots and Stooq (daily US equity/ETF
# closes) still works; the FRED connector is built lazily, so the first FRED
# tool call answers with a `misconfigured` error naming this variable
# instead. Never commit the value.
# FRED_API_KEY=

# TTL of the in-process market-data cache, in whole SECONDS (default 3600).
# Keyed by (source, id, from, to); a warm entry means a /series/download
# issues zero upstream HTTP requests. Note the _SECONDS suffix: every
# duration variable in this codebase holds a plain integer count of seconds,
# never a "5m"-style string.
# WOLF_MARKETDATA_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# ── Sign-in, and Wolf's credential to Orange (api/src/config.ts, api/src/auth/) ──

# Where Agent Orange's agentd answers. In THIS compose stack wolf-api shares
# DinD's network namespace, so agentd is on localhost:8099 — which is the
# default, and why it is not a compose service name (nested session containers
# cannot resolve compose DNS, and neither can a process in DinD's netns).
# A real (non-compose) deployment sets this to agentd's own base URL.
# ORANGE_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8099

# Where a BROWSER reaches Agent Orange. This is the base of the `embed_url`
# that GET /api/hypotheses/:id/embed-token hands the UI, and it is a DIFFERENT
# value from ORANGE_BASE_URL above: that one is agentd as wolf-api sees it
# (localhost:8099, inside DinD's netns, unreachable from a browser), while the
# embed page /embed/session/<name> is served by agent-orange's `web` nginx,
# the only container in that stack publishing a host port. Default 8080, the
# port agent-orange's own compose publishes.
#
# ⚠️ This origin must ALSO be listed in the `wolf` project's `allowed_origins`
# in Orange's AGENTKIT_PROJECT_MAP. The embed page is served with a
# `frame-ancestors` CSP built from that list (go/cmd/agentd/embedcsp.go), so an
# origin missing there means the browser blocks the chat iframe outright — with
# no error on the Wolf side at all.
# ORANGE_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8080

# The "wolf" project's Orange API key, sent as `X-API-Key` on every call
# wolf-api makes to Orange. It is a DIFFERENT credential from WOLF_MCP_TOKEN
# (which authenticates a session container TO wolf-api), and it is what makes
# every memory Wolf writes carry EMPTY provenance — the whole trust model
# rests on that. On the Orange side, name this variable in the project map's
# object form (`"api_key_env": "WOLF_API_KEY"`); configuring a project API key
# is also exactly what turns Orange's dev-open mode off.
#
# REQUIRED: wolf-api refuses to boot without it. Never commit the value.
WOLF_API_KEY=

# Who may sign in: comma-separated, case-insensitive, whitespace-trimmed FULL
# Google addresses. A bare domain (@badcode.dev) or a wildcard (*) is refused
# at boot rather than quietly widening — or emptying — the list.
#
# REQUIRED: unset or empty is a boot failure, deliberately. An empty allowlist
# must never silently mean "every Google account Orange can verify": Orange
# verifying a token is necessary, never sufficient (it mints no token, grants
# no project and owns no user list — Wolf's allowlist is the only gate).
WOLF_ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# The key that signs the `wolf_session` cookie (cookie-parser's built-in
# signing; Wolf holds no server-side session state, so there is no session
# store). At least 32 characters.
#
# REQUIRED: wolf-api refuses to boot without it. Changing it signs everyone
# out, which is also how you revoke every live session. Generate one, and
# never commit the value:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
WOLF_SESSION_SECRET=

# A test-only login, "email:password", which mounts POST /api/auth/dev-login
# and skips Google entirely (owner decision B6). e2e signs in through it
# because Playwright cannot obtain a real Google ID token offline.
#
# OPTIONAL, and REFUSED at boot alongside NODE_ENV=production — the route
# verifies no credential, so a production process that mounts it is a sign-in
# bypass for anything that can reach the port. Set NODE_ENV=development (or
# test) in any stack that uses it. The address must ALSO be on
# WOLF_ALLOWED_EMAILS: this skips Google, never the allowlist.
# WOLF_TEST_LOGIN=

# ── The wolf project bootstrap (api/src/bootstrap/, scripts/bootstrap-project.ts) ──

# The base image every session in the "wolf" Orange project launches from —
# written into that project's `base_image` setting by the (idempotent)
# bootstrap script. Build it with ./scripts/load-image-into-dind.sh first
# (see installations/wolf/Dockerfile); the tag it produces by default is
# the same as this variable's default, so most deployments never set this.
# WOLF_BASE_IMAGE=agent-wolf:dev

# The cron for the project-level "critic" worker's weekly schedule — the job
# that reads recent research notes and rewrites each hypothesis's daily
# researcher method. A plain 5-field cron expression, never a nickname:
# Orange's schedule store rejects "@weekly" outright. Default is Mondays at
# 04:00 UTC.
# WOLF_CRITIC_CRON=0 4 * * 1

# ── Go-live and teardown (api/src/hypothesis/provision.ts) ───────────────────

# The cron for a hypothesis's own daily "researcher-<id>" schedule, created
# at go-live (never at draft). A plain 5-field expression, never a nickname:
# Orange validates `cron` on write and refuses "@daily" outright, and a bad
# value here would fail in the middle of provisioning — after the locked spec
# memory has already been appended, which memories cannot take back. Default
# is 06:00 every day. Overridable so an e2e run can use "* * * * *" and see a
# tick without waiting a day.
# WOLF_SCHEDULE_CRON=0 6 * * *

# How long teardown waits, in whole SECONDS (default 60), for this
# hypothesis's already-queued deliveries to drain after its schedule has been
# deleted — deliveries are read-only over HTTP, there is no cancel route, so
# waiting is the only option. After this bound teardown proceeds anyway and
# logs the delivery ids it left behind. Note the _SECONDS suffix: a plain
# integer count of seconds, never a "60s"-style string.
# WOLF_TEARDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS=60

# How often the evaluation poller sweeps every `live` hypothesis, in whole
# SECONDS (default 300). Each sweep re-reads each hypothesis's datasets
# (version-gated: unchanged data is never re-downloaded), re-evaluates its
# invalidation conditions, writes the board's numbers as a `kind=evaluation`
# memory when they change, and trips `live` -> `challenged` when a condition
# fires or the horizon is reached. This poller is the ONLY mechanism that
# moves a hypothesis to `challenged`: Orange cannot call Wolf, so Wolf polls.
# The interval is started after the HTTP listener, never at import. Between 1
# and 86400.
# WOLF_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# ── The report layer (api/src/report/) ───────────────────────────────────────

# The byte budget for ONE report template, in whole BYTES (default 512000).
# A template is an HTML fragment a human reviews at go-live and then LOCKS:
# after that it is re-read on every frame render and can only be replaced
# through an amendment with a rationale. The cap is what stops an interview
# from locking half a megabyte of inlined chart library into the scoreboard.
#
# BYTES, not characters — the parser measures the utf8 length, so a template
# full of € signs is bigger than it looks. Over the limit is a validation
# error naming both the limit and the actual size; the template is refused,
# never truncated.
# WOLF_REPORT_MAX_BYTES=512000

# The per-metric cap on points injected into the report frame (default 5000).
# Each metric's series is downsampled to at most this many points (the first
# and last are always kept, so the newest move is never the one dropped)
# before being inlined into the frame document as window.__WOLF_SERIES__.
# It bounds the size of every frame render, not just the first.
# WOLF_SERIES_MAX_POINTS=5000

# ── The browser bundle (web/src/env.ts, baked in at BUILD time) ──────────────

# ⚠️ These two are BUILD ARGS, not runtime environment. wolf-web is a static
# nginx image: vite replaces every `import.meta.env.VITE_*` with a string
# literal when the bundle is built, so changing either value needs
# `docker compose up -d --build wolf-web` — restarting the container does
# nothing at all, and nothing fails to tell you so.

# Where a BROWSER reaches Agent Orange, for the chat rail's iframe src
# (`${VITE_ORANGE_PUBLIC_URL}/embed/session/hyp-<id>#token=…`).
#
# ⚠️ It must be the SAME ORIGIN as ORANGE_PUBLIC_URL above — that one is what
# wolf-api puts in the `embed_url` it returns, this one is what the browser
# actually loads — and it must appear in the `wolf` project's
# `allowed_origins` in Orange's AGENTKIT_PROJECT_MAP, because the embed page
# is served with a `frame-ancestors` CSP built from that list. Nothing checks
# that the two variables agree; a mismatch shows up as a blank rail with a CSP
# error in the browser console and no error on the Wolf side at all.
VITE_ORANGE_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8080

# The Google Identity Services client id the sign-in page initialises with.
# Empty is a legitimate state: the sign-in page then renders no Google button
# and says why, and an offline stack signs in through POST /api/auth/dev-login
# (mounted only when WOLF_TEST_LOGIN is set above). Not a secret — a GIS client
# id is public by design; the SECRET half never comes near this repo.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
